anime.js (https://github.com/juliangarnier/anime), MIT License.
runtime/kit/split.js and runtime/kit/scramble.js port its text tools, rewritten in our own ES modules for
plain text and as pure functions of progress: the text splitter's word and grapheme segmentation (words from
the platform's segmenter with closing punctuation glued to the word before and opening punctuation to the
word after, characters kept whole), the space kept as a no-break space, the line rule (a word lower than
the one before by more than half its height starts a line), the clip box around a unit, the twin of a unit
for a slot-roll, and the hidden copy of the original text; and the scramble's reveal schedule (each glyph's
window ranked by position, spaced by the settle share, ending on a step boundary, with a jitter on both ends,
a direction that follows the text's length, the extra glyphs of a shorter text held to go first, and the
named glyph pools with their x-y ranges). Its stateful random stream, its timers and its HTML strings are not
used: noise is a hash of the glyph's place and step, and every unit is built with DOM calls.

The ease families and their in / out / inOut / outIn derivations, the elastic and bounce forms, steps, the piecewise
linear stops and the irregular ease (runtime/kit/ease.js; irregular is seeded), the spring's initial-velocity
equation (checked against its settle times), and the stagger geometry, with its grid, ratio and 3D origins, `use`,
`total`, jitter ramp and order of operations (runtime/kit/stagger.js; the shuffle is seeded) are adapted from its
easings and stagger modules. Its spring is adapted from Apple's WebKit spring demo and its Penner forms from Robert
Penner's easing equations; its bezier from BezierEasing by Gaetan Renaudeau.

